---
title: Pacman Hooks
order: 9
description: alpm hooks, /etc/pacman.d/hooks
tags: [pacman, hooks, alpm, trigger, transaction]
---

ALPM hooks let you run scripts automatically before or after pacman
transactions. Useful for cache cleanup, rebuilds, notifications, and
custom post-install actions.

@code bash
# ---- hook locations ----

# system hooks (from packages, do not edit)
/usr/share/libalpm/hooks/

# user hooks (create your own here)
/etc/pacman.d/hooks/

# set in /etc/pacman.conf:
# HookDir = /etc/pacman.d/hooks/

# create the directory if it doesn't exist
mkdir -p /etc/pacman.d/hooks

@text
Hook file format: INI-style with a [Trigger] and [Action] section.
Files must have the .hook extension.

@code bash
# ---- hook anatomy ----

# /etc/pacman.d/hooks/example.hook

[Trigger]
Operation = Install
Operation = Upgrade
Operation = Remove
Type = Package
Target = *

[Action]
Description = Running custom hook...
When = PostTransaction
Exec = /usr/local/bin/my-script.sh

@text
Trigger fields:

- `Operation`: Install, Upgrade, Remove (can specify multiple)
- `Type`: Package or File
- `Target`: package name, file path, or glob pattern
  - `*` matches all packages
  - `linux` matches only the linux package
  - `usr/lib/*.so` matches shared library files

Action fields:

- `Description`: shown during transaction
- `When`: PreTransaction or PostTransaction
- `Exec`: command to run
- `Depends`: packages the hook needs to run
- `AbortOnFail`: if set, abort transaction on hook failure (PreTransaction only)
- `NeedsTargets`: if set, matching targets are passed via stdin

@code bash
# ---- practical hook examples ----

# 1. Clean package cache after upgrades
# /etc/pacman.d/hooks/clean_cache.hook
[Trigger]
Operation = Upgrade
Operation = Install
Operation = Remove
Type = Package
Target = *

[Action]
Description = Cleaning pacman cache (keeping last 2)...
When = PostTransaction
Exec = /usr/bin/paccache -rk 2
Depends = pacman-contrib

@code bash
# 2. Update pkgfile database after sync
# /etc/pacman.d/hooks/pkgfile_update.hook
[Trigger]
Operation = Install
Operation = Upgrade
Type = Package
Target = *

[Action]
Description = Updating pkgfile database...
When = PostTransaction
Exec = /usr/bin/pkgfile --update
Depends = pkgfile

@code bash
# 3. Rebuild bat cache when syntax files change
# /etc/pacman.d/hooks/bat_cache.hook
[Trigger]
Operation = Install
Operation = Upgrade
Operation = Remove
Type = File
Target = usr/share/bat/syntaxes/*

[Action]
Description = Rebuilding bat syntax cache...
When = PostTransaction
Exec = /usr/bin/bat cache --build
Depends = bat

@code bash
# 4. Warn about .pacnew files
# /etc/pacman.d/hooks/pacnew_warn.hook
[Trigger]
Operation = Upgrade
Type = Package
Target = *

[Action]
Description = Checking for .pacnew files...
When = PostTransaction
Exec = /usr/bin/find /etc -name "*.pacnew" -newer /etc/pacman.d/hooks/pacnew_warn.hook -print

@code bash
# 5. Rebuild initramfs after kernel/microcode updates
# /etc/pacman.d/hooks/initramfs.hook
[Trigger]
Operation = Install
Operation = Upgrade
Type = Package
Target = linux
Target = linux-lts
Target = amd-ucode
Target = intel-ucode

[Action]
Description = Rebuilding initramfs...
When = PostTransaction
Exec = /usr/bin/mkinitcpio -P

@code bash
# ---- list active hooks ----

ls /usr/share/libalpm/hooks/              # system hooks
ls /etc/pacman.d/hooks/                   # user hooks

# see what hooks ran during last transaction
grep -i "hook" /var/log/pacman.log | tail -20

@text
Notes:
- User hooks in /etc/pacman.d/hooks/ override system hooks with the same
  filename.
- Hooks run in alphabetical order by filename. Prefix with numbers
  (01-clean.hook, 02-rebuild.hook) to control order.
- PreTransaction hooks with AbortOnFail can prevent package operations.
- The Exec command runs as root. Ensure scripts are secure and not
  world-writable.
